.TH "TONE-SURVEY" "1" "November 24, 2026" "ka9q-radio" "ka9q-radio Manual"
.SH NAME
tone-survey \- what tone or digital IDs each surveyed frequency actually carries
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tone-survey
.RI "[\-o results.csv] [\-v] group ..."
.SH DESCRIPTION
tone-survey listens to radiod channels opened without tone squelch (mode
mixed: analog FM and every digital mode), normally those activity(1)'s
on-demand audit opens as frequencies are used, and judges each
transmission as it ends: the CTCSS tone found in most of it (55 tones,
looked at every 0.24 s), else the DCS code found most; else, with no tone,
.B voice
(someone talking: a peaky spectrum, harmonics and formants, in many loud
frames, rising and falling with the syllables),
.B carrier
(an empty one: never loud above 300 Hz) or
.B noise
(hiss, steady or pulsing, flat in spectrum; or a steady whistle or data
tone); or, for
a digital call, its mode and IDs (P25 NAC, talkgroup; DMR, NXDN...), split
where radiod says each call begins. Under half a second isn't judged; with
no tone, code or call, under 1.5 s isn't counted.
.P
The results, one row per frequency: transmissions, first and last heard,
the commonest, and everything heard with counts, rewritten every minute
and at exit. frequency-survey.py \-\-tones checks a candidate list's listed
tones against them.
.P
A group is named as radiod names it: the address made from the name, so
nothing need advertise it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "\-o " results.csv
Where the results go (default tone-survey.csv).
.TP
.B "\-v"
Print each transmission as it's judged.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
tone-survey \-o ~/tone-survey.csv tone-survey.local
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
activity(1), radiod(8)
